{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790324889,"nonce":"3Wx4TH1tZ17I4ld5zla9ZuK5M00=","message":"Cause: a Void parameter shifted every later argument when a Cranelift caller entered an LLVM entry.\n\n`main(arg)` in crypto_pyaes binds `o = benchmark()` (None) in its loop, so the header carries a void phi and the region outlined for the interpreted frame (`main$resume0`) takes `(i64, f64, void, f64, i64, *List)`. Cranelift lowers a Void parameter as an i8 in its own register; the LLVM entry declared it as `{}`, which takes no register. Once the region was promoted before the frame left (the warm-up worker path at 16 header visits, so only with -n \u003e= 16), the Cranelift adapter called the LLVM region with rdi=i, rsi=void byte, rdx=n, rcx=times, and the region read n as the list and returned it. `run_benchmark` then iterated a box whose data was 20: zb_any_iter -\u003e zb_list_to_any_f64(20) -\u003e load at 0x1c. The faulting frames are the interpreter calling zb_any_iter natively, not JIT code of the region itself.\n\nFix: the LLVM entry convention passes a Void as the byte Cranelift passes (entry_fn_type/direct_type), the body still holds it as `{}`, cross-tier calls from LLVM pass a zero byte, and the region re-entry helper does not load one from the frame.\n\nReducer: crates/zyntax_python/tests/fixtures/void_loop_value.py (crashes 8/10 on the NUC before, 10/10 correct after). Compiler test: crates/compiler/tests/void_param_abi.rs (LLVM entry called by Cranelift and LLVM calling Cranelift through the cell).","files":null}]}